 what she called the socio Tay and a neem  well ten years before that had  established it the Soviet anonym was an  organization she founded with her  partner's man ray and Marcel Duchamp and  she she was a German immigrant in  Brooklyn began buying hand over fist  works of Modern Art that um at that time  did not take a wealthy person to buy by  the hundreds and Marcel Duchamp to make  a very long story short um is the one  who had vised her in 1953 when she died  to leave that whole collection to Yale  University since her utopian dreams of  making it into a real museum of modern  art in new york city didn't come through  but the whole concept of that kind of  collection started with her because she  ended up moving to Connecticut ended up  at Yale and it's all those works that  are on the walls today I paid a visit  before I came down here um great great  works by all of the classical heroes of  Modern Art who at that time quickly  became if they had not been already my  heroes the kind of thing that we did in  my classes to prepare exhibitions is  something very few graduate schools of  our history can offer you for example  one thing I remember very well is going  to a museum studies class led by Alan  chest at the director of the art museum  the art gallery and we all had as an  assignment to write an essay on a  painting in the collection and that sa  would appear in  book called something like selections  from the yale university art gallery  which was nothing to him a lot of  publications like that coming go during  the course of the years but for me it  was an immense experience mine was  Picasso's the first step this mother  cubist mother holding this crazy cubist  baby and you had to write something like  300 or 400 words on the painting and I  did it and I remember reading it to my  roommate a medical student um by the way  who although I didn't marry her she is  to this day my best friend and she just  lighting up like the fourth of July and  saying WO