 yet but at that point  that was considered a no-no and yet even  though it was a field that my advisor  himself knew very little about he told  me a great anecdote about the one time  he met Joseph voice when he was in  bizzle Dorf and said go ahead and that  freedom was something that I appreciate  still a great great deal the legacy of  the yale art gallery is unique in terms  of not only the art history program here  but the way it interacts with the school  of art and architecture of course and  just this morning at MoMA I had a visit  from an art history alumna from Yale who  had been in the program at the same time  that Richard Serra was a Joseph Albert  student josef albers student in the art  school and she brought me a collage that  Richard that she had bought out of the  student art show on made out of leaves  literally autumn leaves with some paint  smushed around holding the leaves  together and she had a note of  authentification from sarah saying yeah  i did this in 1962 at yale and she said  you know would we like it for a momen to  go with the two you know 17-ton steal  things by richard serra that also are  there  and you know absolutely this was  wonderful but you know I thought wow  that's pretty amazing coincidence that  i'm on my way to New Haven and here's  this but also at the moment for example  i'm working with class oldenburg for a  show on his work of the 60s that will be  at MoMA next year and some of you may  remember in nineteen-sixty-nine when he  put his lipstick on caterpillar tracks  up here at Yale that you may not know  was his very first outdoor monument and  it was at the invitation of the  architecture grad students it together  with Vin Scully who called this guy in  New York who they knew had done  wonderful proposals for these public as  he call the monuments for a few years  but never had occurred to him that  someone would actually invite him to  build one um so it happened to great  fanfare and of course great notoriety  here at Yale but he talks about it so  vividly today 40 some years later that  it's an astounding reminder of the  legacy of this place not only on the  students who went here but so many  artists who the art gallery or the art  program were affected by so that's a  very present kind of way in which the  Yale experience doesn't leave me
 

